[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: pc1
diagnosis_text:
- Affected component: pc1’s NIC transmit path (eth0) at the kernel/driver level, not the inter-AS routing path or policies.
- Mechanism: Frames are being dropped locally on pc1 before they ever leave the host. This manifests as timeouts/retransmissions for flows sourced from pc1, which users perceive as packet loss to the partner network. Because drops occur below Linux qdisc, routers and intermediate links appear clean.
- Localization: pc1 eth0. Evidence: ifconfig on pc1 shows TX dropped 167 on eth0 while tc qdisc (fq_codel) reports dropped 0, proving the loss is below qdisc (driver/NIC ring). An iperf from pc1→pc2 simultaneously showed 167 TCP retransmissions, matching the TX drop counter. In contrast, pc2 and both routers show zero drops, clean iptables, established BGP, and consistent 0% loss in repeated ICMP tests in both directions.
- Root cause: pc1’s eth0 transmit queue/driver was dropping packets (likely a wedged or overflowed veth/NIC ring), causing intermittent loss seen by the user.
- Corrective fix: Restart pc1’s network interface (or the pc1 container/VM). Recommended: on pc1, bounce the interface (ip link set eth0 down; ip link set eth0 up) or restart networking on pc1. No changes are needed on router1/router2; inter-AS BGP and forwarding are healthy.

=== END ===